.TH CVEC_INIT_MP 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cvec_init_mp \- create the vector behind a declared handle with a custom allocator
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cvector.h>

cvec_init_mp(v, mprocs);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This macro creates a vector and stores it in v exactly as cvec_init(3)
does, except that the vector takes every allocation that it makes from
mprocs instead of from the default allocator. cvec_declare(3) or
cvec_declare_scoped(3) must declare v first, and the element size comes
from the element type in that declaration.

The vector keeps a private copy of the procedure table, so the
ccol_memmgmt_procs_t object here does not need to live longer than the
vector, although the functions in that table do. When the vector is
destroyed, it frees the backing buffer, the copy of the table and the
container itself through those functions. A NULL mprocs is the same as a
call to cvec_init(3).

The new vector holds no elements and starts at the minimum capacity of 4.
The capacity doubles when a push finds the buffer full and halves when the
element count falls below a quarter of it, but it never falls below that
minimum of 4.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
v       Name of the vector variable for the new vector, which must be a
        plain identifier. cvec_declare(3) or cvec_declare_scoped(3) must
        declare it first, and its companion type variables must be in
        scope.
mprocs  Pointer to a ccol_memmgmt_procs_t. Its malloc, calloc, realloc
        and free members must all be set. Pass NULL for the default
        allocator.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
None. The macro expands to a statement, not to an expression.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The macro stops the program through ccol_fatal_err() when it cannot
create the vector, with a message that names v and the error below it.
To handle a failed creation yourself, use cvector_create_full(3)
directly; that function does not stop the program.
The macro rejects a procedure table with any of its four members left as
NULL, and that rejection is one of the failures that stops the program
here.
The macro expands to a do/while block and needs a semicolon at the end of
the call site.
The macro evaluates v more than once and pastes v into the names of the
companion variables, so v must be a plain identifier and never an
expression. The macro evaluates mprocs one time.
cvector_get_mprocs(3) reads back the table that created a vector.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
ccol_memmgmt_procs_t procs = {
    .malloc = my_malloc, .calloc = my_calloc,
    .realloc = my_realloc, .free = my_free,
};

cvec_declare(my_vec, int);
cvec_init_mp(my_vec, &procs);
cvec_push(my_vec, 42);
cvec_destroy(my_vec);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cvec_init (3),
.BR cvec_declare (3),
.BR cvec_declare_scoped (3),
.BR cvec_construct_mp (3),
.BR cvec_destroy (3),
.BR cvector_create_full (3),
.BR cvector_get_mprocs (3)
